#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497478} :activity/id #uuid "8b065634-803f-5739-9336-86488fa97262" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817884f4-d5d2-5108-918e-d0ab3bb3b2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{t}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319784} {:answer/id #uuid "86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20t^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496936} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3fa6845-810a-5de2-b921-0cdbf047d2a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb46a1e6-2768-5b65-8fa6-a9b43ac0ddde" :question/math-academy-id "q-350551" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1)+({t}^{5}-(-{t}^{5})) \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2{t}^{5})=20{t}^{9}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958e50e4-711b-576c-ab91-0087c75945cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8fed5f-0e8b-5ec1-8918-492f6fc7c6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} :answer-field/id #uuid "5372760e-066f-55e0-b7b9-2ff11d55e2c0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832c18d4-aaf4-5e10-9aeb-143c9e0d4b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466335} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef8b21-0c2d-5d7e-bac0-f3e5033cf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aeac474-d1d6-5a95-a8c4-6a2ce80af73e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467011} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800c1d5b-5d46-5618-b8ef-1b9451c13e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466337} {:answer/id #uuid "4250fbe7-e751-5a5c-88fa-cbb83498f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7db462d-3fc8-5643-b099-4624769f01bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "757f1645-7b5c-5866-81f6-7ae1eefa124d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-2\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496861} :answer-field/id #uuid "624d06da-e063-516f-b272-d7b29fea68c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" :question/math-academy-id "q-334970" :question/problem "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a22ab5cf-13df-5925-8c56-24a2e18fabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff310a56-8a6f-5ed7-bf98-61e53df7dc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6103b29-3311-5514-bfba-8a71af2fd5aa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ebac1c-34c4-5c6d-bb6c-785ac2249a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466341} {:answer/id #uuid "945abe1f-1a4c-598a-b831-113ac0528de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf27f1b-1445-5c81-aae4-f7f07eb27092" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9bbcda9-a787-548e-a44b-7fe7cb161bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466343} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91fc8-2c32-5a9f-956a-db0acb298dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbdaacc-92a5-5c61-9124-f11f072369ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7fa63bd-d15a-5bf7-b7e2-ddd1185810da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497445}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496653}]}
